# ferrolink

**Fast, parallel link checker for static HTML builds.**

<!-- mdtheme:badges:start -->
[![CI](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferrolink/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferrolink/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![License: Apache-2.0](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-Apache--2.0-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
[![MSRV](https://img.shields.io/badge/rust-1.96%2B-orange.svg)](Cargo.toml)
<!-- mdtheme:badges:end -->

ferrolink reads the output directory of a static site build and checks that
every link, asset reference, and `#fragment` points at something the build
contains. It parses every page in parallel, resolves references the way a
browser does, and maps URLs onto files the way static hosts do. It needs no
server and no network.

It was built to gate releases and deployments of
[Ardo](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ardo) documentation sites, and
works on the output of any static site generator. It ships as a command-line
tool on crates.io and as an npm package with prebuilt native binaries, a
JavaScript API, and a Vite plugin.

```console
$ ferrolink check docs/build/pages --exclude 'v[345]/**'
v6/guide/component-playground/index.html
  line 17    broken link     /v6/storybook

1 issue in 1 file · 318 pages · 10845 internal links checked · 482 external not checked · 24 ms
```

## What it checks

- **Links and assets**: `a`, `area`, and `link` `href`; `script`, `img`,
  `source`, `video`, `audio`, `track`, `iframe`, and `embed` `src`; `srcset`
  candidates; `video` `poster`; `object` `data`; SVG `use` and `image`;
  `meta` refresh targets; `og:image`, `og:url`, and `twitter:image`.
- **Anchors**: a fragment must name an element `id`, or an `<a name>`, on the
  target page. `#top` and text fragments (`#:~:text=`) always resolve.
- **Base path**: on a site served below `/<repo>/`, an absolute link that
  forgets the prefix is reported as `outside base`.
- **Deployment files** at the site root: version paths in Ardo's
  `versions.json`, and redirect and rewrite destinations in `_redirects`
  (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages) and `vercel.json`.

Links inside comments, scripts, styles, and other raw-text elements are
ignored, as a browser ignores them. External URLs are counted, not fetched.

## Install

The command-line tool:

```bash
cargo install --locked ferrolink
```

The development version, straight from the repository:

```bash
cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferrolink
```

For Node.js and Vite, without a Rust toolchain:

```bash
npm install --save-dev ferrolink
```

The npm package brings a prebuilt binary for macOS (arm64, x64), Linux
(arm64, x64; glibc and musl), and Windows (arm64, x64). It needs Node.js
20.19+ or 22.12+, as Vite 7 does.

## Usage

```bash
# A site served from the domain root
ferrolink check docs/build/client

# A GitHub Pages project site, served below /ardo/
ferrolink check docs/build/client --base /ardo/

# Absolute links to your own host (canonical, og:url) are checked too
ferrolink check dist --site-url https://example.org/docs/

# Keep checking links into an archived version, but not the links inside it
ferrolink check docs/build/pages --exclude 'v3/**'

# Accept links to versions that are deployed separately
ferrolink check docs/build/client --assume-present 'v[345]/**'

# Check links and assets, but not #fragments
ferrolink check dist --no-anchors

# Machine-readable output for CI annotations
ferrolink check dist --format json
```

The exit code is `0` when every reference resolves, `1` when issues were
found, and `2` when the check could not run.

`--exclude` and `--assume-present` take globs relative to the build directory.
`--exclude` skips the links *inside* matching pages; `--assume-present`
accepts links *to* matching paths, or to the `index.html` a host serves for
them, without looking for them in the build.

### How URLs map to files

ferrolink follows the rules GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, and most
static servers share. A path ending in `/` serves its `index.html`. Otherwise
the exact file wins, then `<path>.html`, then `<path>/index.html`. Lookups are
case-sensitive, as on those hosts, even on a case-insensitive local disk.

## Node.js

```ts
import { check } from "ferrolink"

const report = await check({
  root: "dist",
  siteUrl: "https://example.org/docs/",
  exclude: ["v3/**"],
})
for (const issue of report.issues) {
  console.log(`${issue.source}:${issue.line} ${issue.kind} ${issue.href}`)
}
```

`check()` takes the options of the command line in camel case (`basePath`,
`siteUrl`, `exclude`, `assumePresent`, `anchors`, `deployFiles`, `threads`)
and resolves with the report `--format json` prints: `summary`, `issues`, and
`timings`. It runs on a worker thread, so the event loop stays free, and
rejects only when the check cannot run. Broken links are issues in the report.

### Vite

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from "vite"
import ferrolink from "ferrolink/vite"

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ferrolink({ siteUrl: "https://example.org/" })],
})
```

The plugin checks the client build output once the build is complete and
fails the build when it finds issues; `level: "warn"` logs them instead. It
takes the options of `check()` except `root`, plus `dir` to check another
directory than the client `build.outDir`. `basePath` defaults to Vite's
`base`. In an app build, where frameworks such as Ardo render their HTML in
`buildApp`, the check runs after every other `buildApp` hook, so it sees the
rendered pages. The plugin works with Vite 7 and 8.

### API stability

The JavaScript API is the supported way to use ferrolink from code. `check()`,
the fields of its options and of the report, the issue kinds, and the
`ferrolink/vite` plugin with its options follow semantic versioning: before
1.0, a breaking change only comes with a new minor version (0.x.0) and is
called out in the changelog. New options, report fields, and issue kinds can
arrive in any minor version; treat an issue kind you do not know as an issue.
The values of `timings` and `origin`, error messages, and log output are not
part of the contract. The Rust library API serves the CLI and the npm binding
and can change in any minor release before 1.0.

## Performance

On the 318-page Ardo documentation build (9.2 MB of HTML, 1,087 files), a full
check takes about 26 ms on an Apple M1 Ultra. The directory walk uses
[ferralk](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferralk); pages are scanned on
every core.

ferrolink scans with [Ferroni](https://github.com/sebastian-software/ferroni)
regular expressions: one pattern finds comments and start tags, a second
splits the attributes of the tags that matter.
[docs/parsers.md](docs/parsers.md) records how it was chosen over the
html5gum, html5ever, and lol_html tokenizers on the same workload.

## Development

```bash
cargo fmt --all --check
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings
cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked
cargo run --release -- check path/to/build
```

The npm package lives in `node/`, with the napi-rs binding in `node/native/`:

```bash
cd node
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm test
```

